(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-male-reproductive-quiz-version-3"]={"title":"Male Reproductive System — Anatomy Exam 3 (Set 3)","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"During descent, the testes are guided toward the scrotum by which fibrous cord that fails to elongate as the body grows?","choices":["Gubernaculum testis","The full spermatic cord","The ligamentum teres","The round ligament"],"answer":0,"exp":"The gubernaculum testis holds the testes in place and, because it does not elongate with body growth, pulls the testes inferiorly toward the scrotum. — Slide 9 (Descent of the Testes)"},{"q":"As the testes descend, they pass through which structures connecting the scrotal chambers to the peritoneal cavity?","choices":["The inguinal canals","The obturator foramina","The femoral canals","The ureters"],"answer":0,"exp":"The testes descend through the inguinal canals, which connect the scrotal chambers to the peritoneal cavity. — Slides 9, 14 (Descent of the Testes)"},{"q":"Testicle development within the abdominal cavity is favored by a temperature of about 98.6°F, whereas sperm development requires a temperature about:","choices":["10°F warmer than body temperature","2°F cooler than body temperature","Equal to body temperature","5°F cooler, at 90°F"],"answer":1,"exp":"Sperm development requires ~96.6°F, roughly 2°F cooler than body temperature — the reason the testes reside outside the abdomen. — Slides 10, 19 (Descent of the Testes)"},{"q":"The blood vessels, ductus deferens, and nerves bundled to each descended testis constitute the:","choices":["Spermatic cord","Ejaculatory duct","Pampiniform plexus","Rete testis"],"answer":0,"exp":"These structures bundled to the testis form the spermatic cord, which passes through the inguinal canal. — Slide 9 (Descent of the Testes)"},{"q":"Which superficial smooth muscle wrinkles the scrotal surface when it contracts?","choices":["Bulbospongiosus","Cremaster muscle","Dartos muscle","Ischiocavernosus"],"answer":2,"exp":"The dartos is a superficial smooth muscle whose contraction wrinkles the scrotal skin. — Slide 18 (Muscles of the scrotum)"},{"q":"When the external temperature is cold, contraction of the cremaster muscle serves to:","choices":["Trigger ejaculation via bulbospongiosus contraction","Wrinkle only the scrotal skin via the dartos muscle","Draw the testes closer to the abdomen to keep them warm","Relax the scrotal sac fully to increase surface area"],"answer":2,"exp":"In cold conditions the cremaster contracts to pull the testes toward the abdomen, maintaining the temperature needed for sperm development. — Slide 19 (Muscles of the scrotum)"},{"q":"During ejaculation, powerful contractions of which paired perineal muscles help push semen toward the external urethral orifice?","choices":["Levator ani and coccygeus muscles only","Dartos and cremaster muscles","Rectus abdominis and pyramidalis","Ischiocavernosus and bulbospongiosus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Ejaculation involves powerful contractions of the ischiocavernosus and bulbospongiosus muscles, propelling semen distally. — Slide 67 (Semen release and ejaculation)"},{"q":"The internal pudendal artery, which helps supply the scrotum and perineum, is a branch of which vessel?","choices":["Internal iliac artery","Femoral artery","External iliac artery","Abdominal aorta directly"],"answer":0,"exp":"The internal pudendal artery branches from the internal iliac artery; the external pudendal comes off the femoral, and the inferior epigastric off the external iliac. — Slide 21 (scrotal blood supply)"},{"q":"The testicular veins form a network within the spermatic cord known as the:","choices":["Prostatic plexus","Hypogastric plexus","Vesical plexus","Pampiniform plexus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pampiniform plexus is the venous network of the testicular vein within the spermatic cord, aiding countercurrent heat exchange. — Slide 14 (The Spermatic Cords)"},{"q":"The deep artery of the penis, which engorges erectile tissue, runs in the center of which structure?","choices":["The corpus spongiosum","The corpora cavernosa","The spongy urethra","The glans penis"],"answer":1,"exp":"The deep artery of the penis runs in the center of each corpus cavernosum, filling it with blood during erection. — Slides 62, 65 (The Penis)"},{"q":"Erection of the penis is mediated by activation of which type of nerves?","choices":["Somatic motor nerves only","Sympathetic nerves only","Parasympathetic nerves","Enteric nerves"],"answer":2,"exp":"Parasympathetic activity relaxes arterial smooth muscle, dilating vessels and engorging erectile tissue to produce erection. — Slide 66 (Erection of the penis)"},{"q":"Semen emission and ejaculation are driven primarily by which division of the autonomic nervous system?","choices":["Parasympathetic","Sympathetic","Somatic afferent","Enteric"],"answer":1,"exp":"Emission and ejaculation are driven by sympathetic activity (with somatic contraction of perineal muscles for ejaculation). — Slide 67 (Semen release and ejaculation)"},{"q":"Which of the following nerves is listed as supplying the scrotum?","choices":["Vagus nerve","Phrenic nerve","Recurrent laryngeal nerve","Genitofemoral nerve"],"answer":3,"exp":"The scrotum receives sensory/motor supply from the hypogastric plexus, ilioinguinal, genitofemoral, and pudendal nerves. — Slide 22 (scrotal nerve supply)"},{"q":"Approximately what are the typical dimensions of an adult testis?","choices":["About 5 cm long, 3 cm wide, 2.5 cm thick","About 12 cm long and 6 cm wide","About 1 cm long and 0.5 cm wide","About 20 cm long and 10 cm wide"],"answer":0,"exp":"An adult testis is roughly 5 cm long, 3 cm wide, and 2.5 cm thick, weighing 10–15 g. — Slide 7 (The Testes)"},{"q":"The fibrous connective-tissue capsule of the testis that sends septa inward toward the mediastinum is the:","choices":["Tunica albuginea","Tunica vaginalis","Dartos fascia","Cremasteric fascia"],"answer":0,"exp":"The tunica albuginea is the fibrous lining that forms septa converging on the mediastinum of the testis; the tunica vaginalis is the outer serous layer. — Slide 23 (Structure of the Testes)"},{"q":"Testosterone, which drives spermatogenesis and secondary sex characteristics, is produced by which testicular cells?","choices":["The spermatogonial stem cell population","Nurse (Sertoli) cells lining the tubule","Interstitial endocrine (Leydig) cells","Ovarian follicular granulosa cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Interstitial endocrine (Leydig) cells, in the spaces between seminiferous tubules, produce and release testosterone. — Slides 27, 29 (Histology of the Testes)"},{"q":"The nurse cells (Sertoli cells) of the seminiferous tubules secrete inhibin and which protein that keeps testosterone concentrated in the tubules?","choices":["Fibrinogen clotting factor","Hyaluronidase acrosomal enzyme","Seminalplasmin antibiotic protein","Androgen-binding protein (ABP)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Nurse (Sertoli) cells secrete inhibin (controls sperm-formation rate) and androgen-binding protein, which binds testosterone within the tubules. — Slide 36 (Nurse Cells)"},{"q":"The seminal glands (seminal vesicles) contribute approximately what percentage of semen volume?","choices":["About 5 percent","About 20 percent","About 60 percent","About 90 percent"],"answer":2,"exp":"The seminal glands produce about 60% of semen volume, rich in fructose, prostaglandins, and clotting factors. — Slide 55 (The Seminal Glands)"},{"q":"The bulbo-urethral (Cowper's) glands secrete a thick alkaline mucus that primarily serves to:","choices":["Produce testosterone within the testis","Provide fructose for ongoing sperm energy needs","Trigger capacitation in the female tract","Neutralize residual urinary acids in the urethra"],"answer":3,"exp":"Bulbo-urethral glands secrete alkaline mucus that neutralizes urinary acids in the urethra and lubricates the penis tip; ~5% of semen volume. — Slide 57 (The Bulbo-urethral Glands)"},{"q":"The head of a spermatozoon contains the chromosomes and an enzyme-filled cap called the:","choices":["Centriole","Acrosome","Flagellum","Mitochondrial spiral"],"answer":1,"exp":"The sperm head contains the chromosomes and the acrosome, which holds enzymes (e.g., hyaluronidase) needed to fertilize the egg. — Slide 38 (Anatomy of a Sperm)"},{"q":"Which sequence correctly traces sperm from formation to the epididymis inside the testis?","choices":["Efferent ductules → rete testis → straight tubules → seminiferous tubules","Rete testis → seminiferous tubules → epididymis → efferent ductules","Seminiferous tubules → straight tubules → rete testis → efferent ductules → epididymis","Epididymis → rete testis → straight tubules → seminiferous tubules"],"answer":2,"exp":"Sperm pass from seminiferous tubules → straight tubules → rete testis (in the mediastinum) → efferent ductules → epididymis. — Slide 25 (Histology of the Testes)"},{"q":"Before entering the prostate, the ductus deferens expands into a dilated region called the:","choices":["Rete testis","Ampulla","Bulb","Crus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ductus deferens expands into the ampulla, which joins the seminal gland's excretory duct to form the ejaculatory duct. — Slide 48 (The Ductus Deferens)"},{"q":"The ejaculatory duct, formed by the union of the ampulla and the seminal gland duct, empties into the:","choices":["Membranous urethra","Prostatic urethra","Spongy urethra","Epididymis"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ejaculatory duct empties into the prostatic urethra. — Slide 48 (The Ductus Deferens)"},{"q":"Sperm become fully capable of fertilizing an egg only after capacitation, which is completed:","choices":["In the female reproductive tract","Within the head of the epididymis","In the seminiferous tubules","In the prostate gland ducts"],"answer":0,"exp":"Capacitation occurs outside the epididymis: sperm become motile when mixed with seminal secretions and gain fertilizing capability in the female tract. — Slide 45 (capacitation)"},{"q":"The middle piece of a spermatozoon is packed with which organelles to power the tail?","choices":["Golgi stacks","Ribosomes","Lysosomes","Mitochondria"],"answer":3,"exp":"The middle piece (with the neck) contains mitochondria and centrioles; the tail is the only flagellum in the human body. — Slide 38 (Anatomy of a Sperm)"},{"q":"Which region of the male urethra passes through the prostate gland?","choices":["Spongy urethra","Membranous urethra","Prostatic urethra","Penile urethra"],"answer":2,"exp":"The prostatic urethra extends from the urinary bladder through the prostate gland. — Slide 51 (The Urethra)"},{"q":"The membranous urethra is distinguished anatomically because it passes through the:","choices":["The prostate gland surrounding the bladder neck","Muscular wall of the pelvic cavity (urogenital diaphragm)","The corpus spongiosum along the penile shaft","The epididymis along the posterior testis"],"answer":1,"exp":"The membranous urethra passes through the muscular wall of the pelvic cavity (the urogenital diaphragm), between the prostatic and spongy portions. — Slide 51 (The Urethra)"},{"q":"The spongy (penile) urethra is enclosed within which erectile column?","choices":["Crus of the penis","Corpus cavernosum","Corpus spongiosum","Tunica albuginea"],"answer":2,"exp":"The spongy urethra runs within the corpus spongiosum, which surrounds it along the length of the penis. — Slides 51, 62 (Urethra / Penis)"},{"q":"The three regions of the male urethra, in order from the bladder outward, are:","choices":["Prostatic, spongy, membranous","Spongy, membranous, prostatic","Membranous, prostatic, spongy","Prostatic, membranous, spongy"],"answer":3,"exp":"From the bladder outward the male urethra is prostatic, then membranous, then spongy (penile). — Slide 51 (The Urethra)"},{"q":"Which portion of the male urethra is the shortest, passing through the urogenital diaphragm?","choices":["The full penile urethra","Prostatic urethra","The full spongy urethra","Membranous urethra"],"answer":3,"exp":"The membranous urethra is the short segment passing through the muscular pelvic floor (urogenital diaphragm), between prostatic and spongy portions. — Slide 51 (The Urethra)"}]};
